.TH zmaze "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zmaze \- GUI maze crawler
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzmaze [\fIoptions\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zmaze -- GUI maze crawler
Usage:	zmaze [ options ]
Keys:	w/s or up/down walk, a/d or left/right turn, F11 help, q quit
.HE
.B zmaze
draws a first-person view of a walled maze and lets you walk it:
walls stand full height around you, shown in perspective and dithered
lighter the closer they are.
Find your way around; there is nothing to collect and no monster to
meet \- the walk itself is the game.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop and accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
.PP
Movement is by keyboard, a step or a turn at a time; held keys repeat,
which makes the motion continuous:
.DS
.ta 4n 22n
	\fBw\fR, \fIup arrow\fR	walk forward
	\fBs\fR, \fIdown arrow\fR	walk backward
	\fBa\fR, \fIleft arrow\fR	turn left
	\fBd\fR, \fIright arrow\fR	turn right
	\fBF11\fR	the help window (also the menu's Help entry)
	\fBq\fR	quit (also the menu's Quit entry)
.DE
Each step is a quarter of a maze cell; each turn is a sixteenth of a
full circle, so sixteen presses of
.B d
face you back the way you came.
You cannot walk through walls: a blocked step slides along the wall
instead, which helps in tight corners.
.PP
A middle click on the window replays the current selection as
keystrokes, one character per key.
Select a string of movement letters anywhere \- or load one with
.B hrclip
\- and middle-click the maze to walk the recorded route.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B zview
.SH NOTES
Distance is shown by dither: near walls are light, far walls dark,
and walls faced head-on are one shade lighter than walls seen along
their length.
If the whole view turns a single even gray, you are standing with
your nose against a wall \- turn with
.B a
or
.B d
and the maze comes back.
.PP
The window has a fixed size (the
.B \-S
option is ignored).
The
.B r
key switches between the two renderers the program carries \- wall
projection from a BSP tree (the default) and column raycasting \- which
draw the same scene and differ only in speed; it exists for comparing
the two and is not needed for play.
